  "interpretation": "<table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; width: 70.0549%; height: 168.066px;\" border=\"1\"><colgroup><col style=\"width: 65.7318%;\"><col style=\"width: 34.3203%;\"></colgroup>\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.6133px;\">\n<td>ETHANOL CONCENTRATION IN mg/dL</td>\n<td>REMARKS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.6133px;\">\n<td>&lt;10&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Negative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.6133px;\">\n<td>10-50&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Subclinical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.6133px;\">\n<td>30-120&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Euphoria</td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 33.6133px;\">\n<td>90-250&nbsp;</td>\n<td>Excitement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>180-300</td>\n<td>Confusion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>250-400</td>\n<td>Stupor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>350-500</td>\n<td>Coma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Note:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ethanol is constantly formed endogenously from acetaldehyde which can be generated in situ from the metabolism of&nbsp;pyruvate, threonine, deoxyribose-5-phosphate, phosphor-ethanolamine, alanine and presumably from other&nbsp;substrates. The levels of blood and tissue endogenous ethanol may change as per function of various physiological&nbsp;conditions such starvation, aging, stress</li>\n<li>In addition to alcoholic beverages, ethyl alcohol (ethanol or alcohol) can also be found in high concentrations in a&nbsp;variety of products such as mouthwashes, colognes, candies and medicinal preparations.</li>\n<li>The pharmacological response to blood alcohol levels may vary from individual to individual. The fatal concentration&nbsp;has been reported to be greater than 400.0 mg/dL.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>&nbsp;Use:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Detection of ethanol (alcohol) in blood to confirm prior consumption or administration of ethanol.</li>\n<li>&nbsp;Quantification of ethanol concentration in blood correlates directly with degree of intoxication.</li>\n<li>Diagnosis of Chronic alcoholism - such individuals will require higher levels than indicated above to achieve various&nbsp;states of intoxication.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
